==================================================
Lab 7: Server-Side Template Injection with a Custom Exploit
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A blog site lets a logged-in user choose a preferred display name. That name is placed into a template, so template syntax in it is evaluated.
The template gives access to a user object, which has methods for managing the user's avatar and personal data.
We use those methods to point the avatar at Carlos's SSH key, then delete it.

Goal: delete /home/carlos/.ssh/id_rsa.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

The display name setting stores a value such as user.name in blog-post-author-display.
When a post loads, the template renders that value above each comment, so you see your name.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The saved value is inserted into the template as code.
Closing the expression early with }} lets us call methods on the user object, including setAvatar() and gdprDelete().

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

user.setAvatar(path, mime): Sets the avatar to any file on the server. The error message told us the second argument is the MIME type.
Avatar endpoint: GET /avatar?avatar=<username> returns the file the avatar points to, which is how we read files such as /etc/passwd.
user.gdprDelete(): Deletes the file the avatar points to. Setting the avatar to Carlos's key and calling this method removes the key.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

First, we set the avatar to the target file:

user.name}}{{user.setAvatar('/home/carlos/.ssh/id_rsa','image/jpg')

The original }} closes the expression, and our setAvatar call runs when the post renders.

Then we delete it:

user.name}}{{user.gdprDelete()

The file the avatar points to is removed, which is Carlos's key.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Log in as wiener, post a comment on any blog post, and upload a valid image as your avatar.
Open My account and set the display name to the setAvatar payload above. Load the post to render it.
Set the display name to the gdprDelete payload and load the post again. The lab is marked as solved once the file is deleted.

Optional: to confirm file reads, set the avatar to /etc/passwd with image/jpg, then load /avatar?avatar=wiener.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It logs in as wiener and fetches a CSRF token from the account page.
* It uploads a valid 1x1 PNG as the avatar. This makes the avatar point to a valid image before the template call.
* It posts a comment, since the name only renders next to comments.
* It saves the setAvatar payload as the display name, renders the post, then saves the gdprDelete payload and renders the post again.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The application passes a user-controlled setting into the template engine as code.
Any method exposed on the user object becomes reachable, including methods that read and delete files on the server.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not put user-controlled values into template source. Store a choice, such as an ID, and map it to a fixed value on the server.
Do not expose file-handling methods on objects that templates can reach.
Validate avatar paths on the server, and never let a user choose an arbitrary file path.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
Error messages can disclose method names and argument types, which guides the exploit.
A feature that looks harmless, such as an avatar, can become a file-read or file-delete primitive through template injection.
Never let users control template source, even through a setting.
